WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.433
Welcome to chapter one: algebraic proofs. We begin with a beautiful shortcut, called the binomial theorem.

2
00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:15.500
By the end of this video, you will be able to expand a bracket raised to any power, and pick out any single term you like, without ever multiplying the brackets out by hand.

3
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:23.833
Let's start with something familiar. A plus B, all squared, gives A squared, plus two A B, plus B squared.

4
00:00:24.233 --> 00:00:29.300
Cube it, and you get four terms. Raise it to the fourth power, and you get five.

5
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:40.767
Now ignore the letters, and look only at the numbers in front. One, two, one. One, three, three, one. One, four, six, four, one.

6
00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:45.733
These numbers are not random. They form one of the most famous patterns in mathematics.

7
00:00:46.933 --> 00:00:50.867
This is Pascal's triangle. Every row starts and ends with a one.

8
00:00:51.267 --> 00:01:01.133
And every other number is simply the sum of the two numbers sitting just above it. One plus two makes three. Three plus three makes six.

9
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:10.033
Each row gives the coefficients for one power. The row for power five reads: one, five, ten, ten, five, one.

10
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:16.700
Notice that the row for power N always has N plus one terms. So the row for power ten has eleven numbers in it.

11
00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:26.233
But writing out row after row is slow. What if we want just one number, deep inside the triangle? For that, we need to understand where these numbers come from.

12
00:01:27.433 --> 00:01:32.400
A plus B, to the fifth power, means five identical brackets, multiplied together.

13
00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:39.467
To build a single term, we pick one letter from every bracket, either an A or a B, and multiply our five picks.

14
00:01:39.867 --> 00:01:47.967
Suppose we want the term A cubed, B squared. That means picking B from exactly two of the five brackets, and A from the other three.

15
00:01:48.367 --> 00:01:54.233
How many different ways can we choose those two brackets? Count them, and there are exactly ten.

16
00:01:54.633 --> 00:02:02.633
So A cubed B squared appears ten times, and its coefficient is ten. Mathematicians call this number, five choose two.

17
00:02:03.833 --> 00:02:11.600
We write N choose R like this: a capital C, with N in front and R behind. And there is a quick recipe to calculate it.

18
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:23.367
For five choose three: on top, start at five and count down three numbers. Five times four times three. Underneath, write three times two times one. The answer is ten.

19
00:02:23.767 --> 00:02:33.700
Two special cases are worth remembering. Choosing nothing, or choosing everything, can only be done in one way. So N choose zero, and N choose N, both equal one.

20
00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:48.200
Now we can describe any term in the expansion of A plus B to the power N. The term containing B to the power R is: N choose R, times A to the power N minus R, times B to the power R.

21
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:53.400
Put all of those terms together, and we have the binomial theorem.

22
00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:59.967
Read it from left to right. The power of A starts at N, and steps down by one each time.

23
00:03:00.367 --> 00:03:06.633
The power of B starts at zero, and steps up. In every term, the two powers add up to N.

24
00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:14.633
And the coefficients, N choose zero, N choose one, N choose two, and so on, are exactly the numbers in Pascal's triangle.

25
00:03:15.833 --> 00:03:22.900
Let's use it. Find the coefficient of X to the fourth, in the expansion of two X squared minus three, all to the power seven.

26
00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:31.967
Think of two X squared as our A, and negative three as our B. Keep the minus sign attached. Losing it is the most common mistake.

27
00:03:32.367 --> 00:03:40.767
To get X to the fourth, we need two X squared, squared. So A has power two, which leaves power five for B, because the powers must add up to seven.

28
00:03:41.167 --> 00:03:47.733
Our term is therefore seven choose five, times two X squared, all squared, times negative three to the fifth.

29
00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:56.933
Seven choose five is twenty-one. Two X squared, all squared, is four X to the fourth. And negative three to the fifth is negative two hundred and forty-three.

30
00:03:57.333 --> 00:04:02.467
Multiply them together, and the coefficient is negative twenty thousand, four hundred and twelve.

31
00:04:03.667 --> 00:04:08.733
Next, a full expansion: three X minus two, to the fourth power.

32
00:04:09.133 --> 00:04:15.633
The coefficients come from row four of Pascal's triangle: one, four, six, four, one.

33
00:04:16.033 --> 00:04:23.400
Now attach the powers. Three X counts down from four to zero, while negative two counts up from zero to four.

34
00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:30.767
Simplify each term carefully, watching the signs. Odd powers of negative two stay negative, so the signs alternate.

35
00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:39.667
And we get eighty-one X to the fourth, minus two hundred and sixteen X cubed, plus two hundred and sixteen X squared, minus ninety-six X, plus sixteen.

36
00:04:40.867 --> 00:04:45.933
What if the bracket has three terms instead of two? This is where the multinomial theorem comes in.

37
00:04:46.333 --> 00:04:53.800
The idea is exactly the same. In A plus B plus C, to the power eight, we pick one letter from each of the eight brackets.

38
00:04:54.200 --> 00:05:03.733
To make A to the fourth, B cubed, C, we need four A’s, three B’s, and one C. The coefficient is the number of ways to arrange those eight letters in a row.

39
00:05:04.133 --> 00:05:13.333
That number is eight factorial, divided by four factorial, times three factorial, times one factorial. Which works out to two hundred and eighty.

40
00:05:13.733 --> 00:05:25.267
In general, the coefficient of A to the P, B to the Q, C to the R, is N factorial, over P factorial, Q factorial, R factorial, where P, Q and R add up to N.

41
00:05:26.467 --> 00:05:35.267
Let's make it harder. Find the coefficient of the same term, in the expansion of A, minus two B, plus three C, all to the power eight.

42
00:05:35.667 --> 00:05:44.233
The counting part does not change. It is still two hundred and eighty. But now each B brings a factor of negative two, and each C brings a factor of three.

43
00:05:44.633 --> 00:05:50.633
So we multiply by negative two cubed, which is negative eight, and by three to the power one.

44
00:05:51.033 --> 00:05:57.367
Two hundred and eighty, times negative eight, times three, gives negative six thousand, seven hundred and twenty.

45
00:05:58.567 --> 00:06:07.467
The binomial theorem is also a powerful tool for proofs. Here is a neat trick. Start with the expansion of one plus X, to the power N.

46
00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:14.367
This is true for every value of X. So we are free to substitute any number we like. Let's choose X equals one.

47
00:06:14.767 --> 00:06:21.267
The left side becomes two to the power N. On the right, every power of X turns into one, leaving just the coefficients.

48
00:06:21.667 --> 00:06:30.567
So the numbers in any row of Pascal's triangle always add up to a power of two. One, two, four, eight, sixteen, thirty-two.

49
00:06:30.967 --> 00:06:39.767
Try X equals negative one instead, and the left side becomes zero. That proves that the coefficients, with alternating signs, always cancel out completely.

50
00:06:40.967 --> 00:06:47.933
Let's recap. The binomial theorem expands A plus B to the power N, using the coefficients N choose R.

51
00:06:48.333 --> 00:06:53.633
The multinomial theorem extends the same idea to three or more terms, using factorials.

52
00:06:54.033 --> 00:06:59.900
And substituting clever values of X, into one plus X to the N, proves surprising identities.

53
00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:09.033
Now it is your turn. Pause the video, and find the coefficient of X to the sixth, Y, in the expansion of two X minus three Y, to the power seven.

54
00:07:09.433 --> 00:07:16.900
Did you get negative one thousand, three hundred and forty-four? Well done. In the next video, we will learn how to divide polynomials.

